data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (Cosmos provider), the **sync-over-async opt-in is removed entirely**. The synchronous APIs — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc. — now throw `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally. There is no configuration flag to re-enable them.

### Every method in your repository breaks:

| Sync (broken) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Fix — convert to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- This only affects the **Cosmos DB provider**; the relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) still support sync APIs.
- Callers must be updated to `await` these methods, which typically means propagating `async`/`Task` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove any `CosmosOptionsExtension.EnableSyncOverAsync` or equivalent configuration — it no longer exists and will cause a build error.
